so from Monday some lockdown  restrictions will be easing in England  we just heard about some the measures  that will change in Scotland do you  think it's too soon to be easing the  lockdown there's an awful lot of people  Sophie who are absolutely desperate to  get back to work there are children who  need to go back to school and labour  supports the lockdown easing but it's  critically important that as that  happens it's done in the safest way that  it possibly can be and I think that's  where over the weekend we've seen some  scientists in particular raising  concerns about whether the way the  government is going about this is safe  or not and we need to make sure maximum  safety is always applied so the public  the public can be reassured about what  they're being asked to do is is safe for  them and we'll just try and pin you down  on some of the specifics because of  course heaven wants to make sure that  things are as safe as possible I don't  think anyone would disagree with that  and schools are a right at the top of  the agenda at a minute with more primary  schools in England opening to more  pupils from tomorrow now in last week's  programs Sir Michael Wilshaw the former  head of Ofsted said that keeping kids  away from school risks a lost generation  and undoing some of that progress that's  been made for disadvantaged children in  recent years so does labour support more  primary schools going back from Monday  or not labour supports a gradual  reopening of the schools in a way that  is safe for children for teachers for  everyone else that works in the schools  and it's critical before now parents are  not going to send their kids back to  school if they don't think it's  absolutely safe to do so so there's a  there's an issue here about reassurance  and providing parents with that  information but but where it's possible  to open schools and absolutely we won't  see schools open where you know am i  aware that in some communities up to a  third of children are not getting any  any education at all because they don't  have the technology at home that allows  them to have lessons now two months  three months four months and potentially  for a child to be out of school is an  incredibly big gap in their education so  all of us want to see schools oh but it  has to be done in a gradual way that  maintains parents confidence that it's  safe for their child to go  are you're the shadow community  secretary so you're responsible for  local government I mean is it acceptable  that there's a bit of a piecemeal  approach here with some local  authorities recommending that schools  open some not we're effectively living  in a postcode lottery when it comes to  our children's education actually local  authorities will take decisions that are  appropriate for their areas based on the  science and the data as it applies to  their locality we don't have a single  rate of infection of this disease across  the whole country it's much higher in  some areas than it is in other areas so  the way to deal with that is to set  clear national standards that everybody  can understand in particular localities  well just be clear what what are those  clear national standards then because I  think it's important to be specific and  you saying that if if infections are  above a certain area in one local  government area then it's acceptable for  schools to open what are those national  standards where the rate of infection is  higher as it is in some areas in the  northwest for instance we've seen some  councils I think in the northwest both  labor and conservative talking about not  wanting schools to reopen quite as  quickly because the rate of infection is  higher now the national standards say to  only be done in a way that is safe to  limit the spread of infection now I  think it's quite right to have a set of  national standards like that but local  application based on the different  circumstances in each locality so that  the 